\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces ADM masses of the spherical stars against oscillation frequency at the working couplings. Lower $\omega $ means deeper binding and larger $|M_{\rm  ADM}|$; at equal frequency the phantom star is the heavier in magnitude, its repulsive self-gravity spreading it over more volume. Stars: the campaign pair---canonical at $\omega =0.750$, phantom retuned to $\omega =0.7603$ so the mass magnitudes match to $0.4\%$. Both branches have a \emph  {floor}: no star on either side is lighter than ${\simeq }0.54$ of the campaign mass, and neither branch has a bound star at $\omega =1$---the bound that shapes the mass-scaling ladder of Sec.~\ref {sec:scaling}.}}{3}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:family}{{2}{3}{ADM masses of the spherical stars against oscillation frequency at the working couplings. Lower $\omega $ means deeper binding and larger $|M_{\rm ADM}|$; at equal frequency the phantom star is the heavier in magnitude, its repulsive self-gravity spreading it over more volume. Stars: the campaign pair---canonical at $\omega =0.750$, phantom retuned to $\omega =0.7603$ so the mass magnitudes match to $0.4\%$. Both branches have a \emph {floor}: no star on either side is lighter than ${\simeq }0.54$ of the campaign mass, and neither branch has a bound star at $\omega =1$---the bound that shapes the mass-scaling ladder of Sec.~\ref {sec:scaling}}{figure.2}{}}
