\newlabel{sec:runaway}{{V\,A}{5}{}{section*.12}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The force law over $d_0=8$--$20$ at the base grid. (a)~Fitted acceleration against separation: the power-law fit gives $d^{-2.03\pm 0.01}$; the point-mass line $\bar  M/d^2$ is indistinguishable at this scale. (b)~$a\,d^2$ over the pair mass $\bar  M$: the close-pair excess, $+3.1\%$ where the envelopes overlap most, decays monotonically and is gone by $d=16$---a finite-size correction behaving like one. The open diamond is the converged ($N=256$) value at $d=10$: refining the grid raises every acceleration by ${\sim }7\%$ (Sec.~\ref {sec:convergence}), a calibration common to the whole scan that cancels from the exponent and the trend.}}{6}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:forcelaw}{{5}{6}{The force law over $d_0=8$--$20$ at the base grid. (a)~Fitted acceleration against separation: the power-law fit gives $d^{-2.03\pm 0.01}$; the point-mass line $\bar M/d^2$ is indistinguishable at this scale. (b)~$a\,d^2$ over the pair mass $\bar M$: the close-pair excess, $+3.1\%$ where the envelopes overlap most, decays monotonically and is gone by $d=16$---a finite-size correction behaving like one. The open diamond is the converged ($N=256$) value at $d=10$: refining the grid raises every acceleration by ${\sim }7\%$ (Sec.~\ref {sec:convergence}), a calibration common to the whole scan that cancels from the exponent and the trend}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {B}The force law: $a\,d^2$ returns the stars' mass}{6}{section*.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:forcelaw}{{V\,B}{6}{}{section*.13}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {II}{\ignorespaces The separation scan at the base grid ($N=128$, fits over $5\le t\le 200$). $a\,d^2$ should return the pair mass $\bar  M=0.014322$ at every separation. $|P|$ is the signed total momentum at $t=200$---it halves with each step outward, tracking the acceleration rather than sitting at a diagnostic floor.}}{6}{table.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:forcelaw}{{II}{6}{The separation scan at the base grid ($N=128$, fits over $5\le t\le 200$). $a\,d^2$ should return the pair mass $\bar M=0.014322$ at every separation. $|P|$ is the signed total momentum at $t=200$---it halves with each step outward, tracking the acceleration rather than sitting at a diagnostic floor}{table.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {C}Convergence, and what cannot move the answer}{6}{section*.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{sec:convergence}{{V\,C}{6}{}{section*.14}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces (a)~The resolution ladder at $d_0=10$: midpoint drift at $\Delta x=0.50$, $0.33$, $0.25$, with two twins of the base rung overlaid---the elliptic solve driven $4.4\times $ deeper (circles) and mesh refinement enabled (crosses); both reproduce it to $2\times 10^{-5}$. Inset: the endpoint---the two fine grids agree to $0.4\%$ while the base rung sits $4\%$ low. (b)~Why no formal convergence order exists: the $t=0$ constraint violation \emph  {rises} with resolution (initial-data interpolation noise, $\propto \Delta x^{-2}$) while the evolution's own violation \emph  {falls}; two error sources with opposite grid behaviour never leave a single asymptotic power.}}{7}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:convergence}{{6}{7}{(a)~The resolution ladder at $d_0=10$: midpoint drift at $\Delta x=0.50$, $0.33$, $0.25$, with two twins of the base rung overlaid---the elliptic solve driven $4.4\times $ deeper (circles) and mesh refinement enabled (crosses); both reproduce it to $2\times 10^{-5}$. Inset: the endpoint---the two fine grids agree to $0.4\%$ while the base rung sits $4\%$ low. (b)~Why no formal convergence order exists: the $t=0$ constraint violation \emph {rises} with resolution (initial-data interpolation noise, $\propto \Delta x^{-2}$) while the evolution's own violation \emph {falls}; two error sources with opposite grid behaviour never leave a single asymptotic power}{figure.6}{}}
